\title{Invariance and Washout of a Character Information Channel\\ on Finite Abelian Class Groups}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a mutual-information statistic---the \emph{dial}---attached to a probabilistic sampler whose outputs are labelled by elements of a finite abelian group $G$. Given a \emph{class-rate profile} $s : G \to [0,1]$, the dial $T(s)$ is the mutual information between the class of a two-factor composite and the binary event that both of its factors are usable; it is governed by the averaged convolution $s \star s$ and the binary entropy function. It is known that $T$ is bounded by the universal constant $C^{*} = h(1/4) - \tfrac12 h(1/2) = \tfrac32\log 2 - \tfrac34\log 3 \approx 0.21576$ nats, with equality exactly at indicators of cosets of index-two subgroups. We establish four structural laws. \emph{(i) Inflation invariance:} for any surjective homomorphism $f : G \to Q$ and any profile $s$ on $Q$, $T(s \circ f) = T(s)$, and likewise for the $k$-factor dial $\Phi_k$; hence the dial is constant along the ``regime'' and ``bit-length'' axes of an experimental grid, and every coset of an index-two subgroup, inflated to any larger group, sits exactly at $C^{*}$. \emph{(ii) Washout dichotomy and parity criterion:} if the sampler pre-multiplies by a uniformly random element of a multiplier subgroup $H \le G$, then some admissible $H$-invariant profile attains $C^{*}$ if and only if $H$ lies in an index-two subgroup, if and only if $[G:H]$ is even; the mean rate is preserved exactly by the randomisation, so two profiles may share a mean while their dials differ by the full cap. \emph{(iii) A continuous degradation law:} for the contrast family $s_t = (1 + t\chi)/2$ attached to a quadratic character $\chi$, the conditional no-fork probability is $(1 + t^2\chi(c))/4$---the contrast enters squared---whence $T(s_t) = D(t^2)$ for the group-free function $D(u) = h(1/4) - \tfrac12[h((1+u)/4) + h((1-u)/4)]$, strictly increasing on $[0,1]$ with $D(0)=0$, $D(1)=C^{*}$. \emph{(iv) A budgeted-adversary threshold:} a maximal channel survives every multiplier group of order at most $B$ if and only if $B < 2^{v_2(|G|)}$; the defence depends only on the $2$-part of the class group order. We further prove that channels of arbitrary character order $d \ge 2$ have strictly positive dial $h(1/d^2) - \tfrac1d h(1/d)$ and collapse to exactly zero under multiplier groups generating all residues, and that the $k$-factor dial at a quadratic kernel equals $h(2^{-k}) - \tfrac12 h(2^{-(k-1)}) > 0$ for every $k \ge 2$ while washing out to zero at every $k$. These results explain three experimental observations on fixed-multiplier continued-fraction factoring ladders.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Sieve-style factoring computations spend most of their time producing candidate objects and testing them for a smoothness or splitting condition. Predicting, from a short pilot run, whether a configuration will eventually succeed is of practical value. The default predictor is the \emph{count}: the empirical rate at which candidates pass the test.

Count discards all structural information. In the settings we have in mind, each candidate carries an additional label: the class it occupies in a finite abelian group $G$---the form class group of the relevant discriminant, or the unit group of a modulus. If pass/fail behaviour correlates with the class, then the class label is an information channel, and the strength of that channel is a statistic in its own right.

This paper analyses that statistic---the \emph{dial}---and answers three empirically raised questions: why the dial is nearly constant across sampling regimes and input sizes; why it outperforms the count statistic systematically; and why randomising the algorithm's auxiliary multiplier destroys it completely. The answers are, respectively: because it is an invariant of a character quotient; because there exist profiles with identical counts whose dials differ by the entire admissible range; and because averaging a nontrivial character over a subgroup that escapes its kernel annihilates it exactly.

\subsection{Empirical background}

The motivating experiments ran a fixed-multiplier continued-fraction factoring ladder over a $2 \times 2$ grid: sampling regime (\emph{balanced} or \emph{uniform}) crossed with input bit-length ($44$ or $48$). In each cell the Spearman rank correlation between the dial and the eventual relation rate was measured; the four values were
\[
0.686,\quad 0.656,\quad 0.553,\quad 0.561,
\]
with confidence intervals excluding zero in all four cells, and with the dial beating the count statistic by $+0.06$ to $+0.10$ in each. A companion pilot replaced the fixed multiplier $k=1$ by a randomised multiplier; the channel disappeared entirely, while the measured rate was unaffected. These observations anchor the theory; the theorems themselves are unconditional statements about finite abelian groups.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $G$ is a finite abelian group, written multiplicatively with identity $1$. All entropies are in nats.

\begin{definition}[Binary entropy]
For $x \in [0,1]$, $h(x) = -x\log x - (1-x)\log(1-x)$, with $h(0)=h(1)=0$. The function $h$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ and strictly concave.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Class-rate profile and mean rate]
A \emph{profile} is a function $s : G \to \mathbb{R}$; it is \emph{admissible} if $0 \le s(a) \le 1$ for all $a$. Its \emph{mean rate} (the \emph{count statistic}) is $\bar{s} = |G|^{-1}\sum_{a\in G} s(a)$. The interpretation is that a candidate landing in class $a$ is usable with probability $s(a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Averaged convolution and fork profiles]
For profiles $t,s$ set
\[
(t \star s)(c) = \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{a \in G} t(a)\,s(ca^{-1}).
\]
The \emph{no-fork profile} of $s$ is $N_s = s\star s$; the convolution powers are $s^{\star 1} = s$ and $s^{\star (k+1)} = s^{\star k}\star s$.
\end{definition}

If $A,B$ are independent uniform classes, each independently declared usable with the probability given by $s$, then $N_s(c)$ is the conditional probability that both are usable given $AB=c$; averaging over $c$ returns $\bar{s}^{\,2}$, and more generally the average of $s^{\star k}$ is $\bar{s}^{\,k}$.

\begin{definition}[The dial]
For an admissible profile $s$,
\[
T(s) = h\!\left(\bar{s}^{\,2}\right) - \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{c\in G} h\bigl(N_s(c)\bigr),
\qquad
\Phi_k(s) = h\!\left(\bar{s}^{\,k}\right) - \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{c\in G} h\bigl(s^{\star k}(c)\bigr),
\]
so that $T = \Phi_2$.
\end{definition}

$T(s)$ is exactly the mutual information $I(C;Y)$, where $C$ is uniform on $G$ and, conditionally on $C=c$, $Y$ is Bernoulli with parameter $N_s(c)$, the marginal parameter being $\bar{s}^{\,2}$. In particular $T(s)\ge 0$, with equality iff $N_s$ is constant.

\begin{definition}[Subgroup profile, coset profile, quadratic character]
For $K \le G$ the \emph{subgroup profile} is the indicator $1_K$, and for $x \in G$ the \emph{coset profile} is $a \mapsto 1_K(x^{-1}a)$. If $[G:K]=2$, the \emph{quadratic character} $\chi_K$ takes the value $+1$ on $K$ and $-1$ off $K$; it is a homomorphism $G \to \{\pm 1\}$ and $1_K = (1+\chi_K)/2$.
\end{definition}

We take as given the following two facts, established previously for this channel.

\begin{theorem}[Cap and rigidity]\label{thm:cap}
For every finite abelian $G$ and every admissible $s$,
\[
T(s) \le C^{*} := h\!\left(\tfrac14\right) - \tfrac12 h\!\left(\tfrac12\right) = \tfrac32\log 2 - \tfrac34\log 3 \approx 0.2157615543 \text{ nats} = 0.3112781245 \text{ bits},
\]
with equality if and only if $s$ is the indicator of a coset of a subgroup of index two. Moreover $\Phi_k(s) \le C^{*}$ for every $k$, and for $[G:K]=2$,
\[
T(1_K) = C^{*}, \qquad \Phi_k(1_K) = h\!\left(2^{-k}\right) - \tfrac12 h\!\left(2^{-(k-1)}\right),
\]
while for a subgroup of arbitrary index $d$, $T(1_K) = h(1/d^{2}) - \tfrac1d h(1/d)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity of the cap]
$C^{*} > 0$; numerically $C^{*} \approx 0.21576$ nats. This follows from $\log 2 > 0.6931471803$ and $\log 3 \le \tfrac32\log 2 + \tfrac1{16}$.
\end{lemma}

\section{Inflation invariance: the dial sees only the character quotient}

\begin{lemma}[Fibre counting]\label{lem:fibre}
Let $f : G \to Q$ be a surjective homomorphism of finite abelian groups and $F : Q \to \mathbb{R}$. Then $\sum_{a\in G} F(f(a)) = |\ker f|\sum_{b \in Q} F(b)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $G$ into the fibres of $f$; each is a coset of $\ker f$ of size $|\ker f|$, on which $F\circ f$ is constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Averages are inflation invariant]\label{lem:avg}
With $f$ as above, $\overline{F \circ f} = \bar{F}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fibre} the numerator gains a factor $|\ker f|$; by Lagrange's theorem for a surjection, $|G| = |\ker f|\cdot |Q|$, so the denominator gains the same factor.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Convolution commutes with pull-back]\label{lem:conv}
For profiles $t,s$ on $Q$ and $c \in G$, $\bigl((t\circ f)\star(s\circ f)\bigr)(c) = (t\star s)(f(c))$. Consequently $(s\circ f)^{\star k} = s^{\star k}\circ f$ for every $k \ge 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substituting $f(ca^{-1}) = f(c)f(a)^{-1}$ makes the summand a function of $f(a)$ alone; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:avg}. The statement for powers follows by induction on $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inflation invariance of the dial]\label{thm:inflation}
Let $f : G \to Q$ be a surjective homomorphism of finite abelian groups and $s : Q \to \mathbb{R}$ a profile. Then $T(s\circ f) = T(s)$ and $\Phi_k(s\circ f) = \Phi_k(s)$ for every $k \ge 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The mean-rate term is Lemma~\ref{lem:avg} applied to $s$; the conditional term is Lemma~\ref{lem:avg} applied to $h\circ s^{\star k}$, whose pull-back is $h \circ (s\circ f)^{\star k}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:conv}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Regime invariance]
If $e : G \to Q$ is an isomorphism then $T(s\circ e) = T(s)$: relabelling the class group does not move the dial.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Bit-length invariance]\label{cor:bitlen}
For any finite abelian $Q$ and any profile $s$ on $G$, the profile $(a,q)\mapsto s(a)$ on $G\times Q$ has dial $T(s)$, and likewise for $\Phi_k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The intersection cell]\label{thm:cell}
Let $K \le G$ with $[G:K]=2$, let $x \in G$ be arbitrary and let $Q$ be an arbitrary finite abelian group. Then the profile $(a,q)\mapsto 1_K(x^{-1}a)$ on $G\times Q$ has dial exactly $C^{*}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Corollary~\ref{cor:bitlen} removes the factor $Q$; Theorem~\ref{thm:cap} evaluates the coset indicator at the cap, uniformly in the translate $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:cell} states precisely that the experimental grid is a single cell. Increasing the bit-length enlarges the ambient class group; changing the regime relabels it or translates the profile. Neither alters the character quotient through which the profile factors, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:inflation} the dial is a function of that quotient alone.
\end{remark}

\section{Multiplier randomisation: the washout dichotomy}

\begin{definition}[Multiplier randomisation and invariance]
For $H \le G$ and a profile $s$, set $(\mix_H s)(a) = |H|^{-1}\sum_{h\in H}s(ha)$: the profile seen by a sampler that multiplies its input by a uniformly random $h \in H$. A profile is \emph{$H$-invariant} if $s(ha)=s(a)$ for all $h\in H$, $a\in G$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Basic properties]
For admissible $s$: $\mix_H s$ is admissible; $\mix_H s$ is $H$-invariant; and if $H \le H'$, every $H'$-invariant profile is $H$-invariant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Admissibility is convexity; invariance re-indexes the sum by right translation by an element of $H$, a bijection of $H$; monotonicity is immediate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Count blindness]\label{thm:countblind}
$\overline{\mix_H s} = \bar{s}$ for every profile $s$ and every $H \le G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exchange the order of summation: for fixed $h$, $a\mapsto ha$ is a bijection of $G$, so the average of $s(ha)$ over $a$ is $\bar{s}$. Averaging $|H|$ copies returns $\bar{s}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Total mixing and silence of constants]\label{lem:const}
$\mix_G s$ is the constant profile $\bar{s}$; and for any constant $t$, $T(\mathrm{const}\ t) = 0$ and $\Phi_k(\mathrm{const}\ t) = 0$ for all $k$, because $(\mathrm{const}\ t)^{\star k} = \mathrm{const}\ t^{k}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}[Total washout]
$T(\mix_G s) = 0$ and $\Phi_k(\mix_G s) = 0$ for every profile $s$ and every $k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Washout dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $H \le G$. There exists an admissible $H$-invariant profile $s$ with $T(s) = C^{*}$ if and only if there exists $K \le G$ with $[G:K]=2$ and $H \le K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) By the rigidity half of Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}, a maximiser is a coset indicator $s = 1_{xK}$ with $[G:K]=2$. Then $s(x)=1$, and $H$-invariance gives $s(hx)=1$, i.e.\ $h \in K$ for every $h \in H$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $H \le K$ then $1_K$ is $H$-invariant and attains $C^{*}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Odd index kills the cap]\label{cor:oddindex}
If $[G:H]$ is odd then every admissible $H$-invariant profile satisfies $T(s) < C^{*}$; in particular $T(\mix_H s) < C^{*}$ for every admissible $s$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Attaining the cap would give $K$ of index $2$ with $H \le K$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}, whence $2 \mid [G:H]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Count-blind separation]\label{thm:separation}
Let $[G:K]=2$. Then $\overline{\mix_G 1_K} = \overline{1_K} = \tfrac12$ and
\[
T(1_K) - T(\mix_G 1_K) = C^{*} > 0 .
\]
Two admissible profiles thus exist with the same mean rate whose dials differ by the full admissible range.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Character equidistribution]\label{thm:orth}
Let $[G:K]=2$ with character $\chi$, and let $H \le G$ with $H \not\le K$. Then $\sum_{h\in H}\chi(h) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Pick $h_1 \in H\setminus K$, so $\chi(h_1)=-1$. Right translation by $h_1$ is a bijection of $H$ and $\chi(gh_1) = -\chi(g)$; hence the sum equals its own negative.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One non-residue multiplier flattens the channel]\label{thm:flatten}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:orth}, $\mix_H 1_K \equiv \tfrac12$ and $T(\mix_H 1_K) = 0$, while the mean rate stays at $\tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $1_K = (1+\chi)/2$ and use $\chi(ha)=\chi(h)\chi(a)$:
\[
(\mix_H 1_K)(a) = \tfrac12 + \frac{\chi(a)}{2|H|}\sum_{h\in H}\chi(h) = \tfrac12
\]
by Theorem~\ref{thm:orth}; now apply Lemma~\ref{lem:const}.
\end{proof}

\section{The parity criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Index-two existence]\label{thm:indextwo}
Every finite abelian group $Q$ of even order has a subgroup of index two.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
We avoid the structure theorem. Write $|Q| = 2^{j}m$ with $m$ odd and $j \ge 1$. Sylow's existence theorem provides $M \le Q$ of order $2^{j-1}$, so $A := Q/M$ is abelian of order $2m$. The squaring map $\sigma : A \to A$, $\sigma(x)=x^{2}$, is a homomorphism. Its kernel consists of elements of order dividing $2$, hence is a $2$-group; it is nontrivial by Cauchy's theorem; and its order cannot be $4$ or more since a subgroup order divides $2m$ and $4\nmid 2m$. Therefore $|\ker\sigma|=2$ and $[A:\im\sigma] = |\ker\sigma| = 2$. Pulling $\im\sigma$ back along $Q \to Q/M$ preserves the index.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:evenindex}
For finite abelian $G$ and $H \le G$: there exists $K$ of index two with $H \le K$ if and only if $[G:H]$ is even.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) $|G/H| = [G:H]$ is even, so $G/H$ has an index-two subgroup by Theorem~\ref{thm:indextwo}; its pre-image in $G$ has index two and contains $H$. ($\Rightarrow$) $[G:K]=2$ divides $[G:H]$ when $H \le K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity criterion for washout]\label{thm:parity}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group and $H \le G$. Then there exists an admissible $H$-invariant profile with $T(s)=C^{*}$ if and only if $[G:H]$ is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The two ends of the multiplier scale]
If $|G|$ is even, the trivial multiplier group $H=\{1\}$ (index $|G|$, even) supports a maximal channel---the \emph{fixed multiplier} regime---while $H = G$ (index $1$, odd) drives every profile's dial to exactly $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Arithmetic realisation]
For every odd prime $p$, the class group $(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{\times}$ of even order $p-1$ admits a profile attaining $C^{*}$---the Legendre-symbol coset indicator---while full multiplier randomisation drives every profile to dial value $0$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Partial washout: the degradation law}

\begin{definition}[Contrast family]
For $[G:K]=2$ with character $\chi$ and $t \in \mathbb{R}$, let $s_t(a) = (1 + t\chi(a))/2$. For $|t|\le 1$ this is admissible; $s_1 = 1_K$ and $s_0 \equiv \tfrac12$. Since $\sum_a \chi(a) = 0$, the mean rate is $\bar{s_t} = \tfrac12$ for every $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The contrast enters squared]\label{thm:squared}
For $[G:K]=2$ and every $c \in G$, $N_{s_t}(c) = \bigl(1 + t^{2}\chi(c)\bigr)/4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Using $\chi(ca^{-1}) = \chi(c)\chi(a)$ and $\chi(a)^2 = 1$,
\[
s_t(a)\,s_t(ca^{-1}) = \frac{1 + t^{2}\chi(c)}{4} + \frac{t\,(1+\chi(c))}{4}\,\chi(a),
\]
and averaging over $a$ annihilates the second term.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Degradation law]
For $u \in [0,1]$, $D(u) = h(1/4) - \tfrac12\bigl[h((1+u)/4) + h((1-u)/4)\bigr]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[One-dimensional reduction]\label{thm:onedim}
For $[G:K]=2$ and $|t|\le 1$, $T(s_t) = D(t^{2})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\bar{s_t} = \tfrac12$ gives the first term $h(1/4)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:squared} the conditional term averages $h((1+t^{2}\chi(c))/4)$ over $c$, and $\chi$ takes the values $\pm1$ equally often on an index-two kernel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
$D$ is strictly increasing on $[0,1]$, with $D(0)=0$ and $D(1) = C^{*}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $0 \le u < v \le 1$ and set $\lambda = \tfrac12(1+u/v)\in(0,1)$. Then
\[
\lambda\tfrac{1+v}{4} + (1-\lambda)\tfrac{1-v}{4} = \tfrac{1+u}{4},
\qquad
(1-\lambda)\tfrac{1+v}{4} + \lambda\tfrac{1-v}{4} = \tfrac{1-u}{4}.
\]
Strict concavity of $h$ applied to each convex combination gives two strict inequalities; adding them yields $h((1+u)/4) + h((1-u)/4) > h((1+v)/4)+h((1-v)/4)$, i.e.\ $D(u)<D(v)$. The endpoints are direct computations, using $h(0)=0$ for $D(1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Partial-washout continuum]\label{thm:continuum}
Let $[G:K]=2$ and $0<t<1$. Then $s_t$ is admissible, has the same mean rate as the maximiser $1_K$, and satisfies $0 < T(s_t) < C^{*}$. By continuity and strict monotonicity of $D$, every value in $(0,C^{*})$ is the dial of an admissible profile of mean rate $\tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Subgroup multipliers realise only the endpoints]\label{thm:endpoints}
Let $[G:K]=2$, $H \le G$ and $|t|\le 1$. Then $\mix_H s_t = s_t$ if $H \le K$, and $\mix_H s_t = s_0 \equiv \tfrac12$ if $H \not\le K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $H \le K$ then $\chi(ha) = \chi(a)$ for $h \in H$. Otherwise Theorem~\ref{thm:orth} gives $\sum_{h\in H}\chi(h)=0$ and the computation of Theorem~\ref{thm:flatten} applies with the extra factor $t$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:continuum} and \ref{thm:endpoints} together show that the dichotomy is not a claim that intermediate channel strengths are impossible, but the statement that \emph{subgroup} averaging realises only the two endpoints of a genuine continuum.

\section{Beyond order two and beyond two factors}

\begin{theorem}[Positive dial at every order]\label{thm:orderd}
Let $K \le G$ with $[G:K] = d \ge 2$. Then $T(1_K) = h(1/d^{2}) - \tfrac1d h(1/d) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The value is Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}. Positivity uses the sharpened tangent bounds
\[
-x\log x + x(1-x) \;\le\; h(x) \;\le\; -x\log x + x \qquad (0<x<1),
\]
the lower bound from $\log(1-x)\le -x$ and the upper bound from $\log(1-x) \ge -x/(1-x)$. With $x = 1/d \le 1/2$, the lower bound at $x^{2}$ and the upper bound at $x$ leave the residual $x^{2}\log 2 - O(x^{3}) > 0$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $T(1_K) = 0.21576,\,0.13666,\,0.09321,\,0.06786,\,0.05184$ for $d = 2,\dots,6$.

\begin{theorem}[Collapse of an order-$d$ channel]\label{thm:collapsed}
Let $K \le G$ and $H \le G$ with $H \vee K = G$ (the multipliers hit every residue class modulo $K$). Then
\[
\mix_H 1_K \equiv \frac{1}{[G:K]}, \qquad T(\mix_H 1_K) = 0, \qquad \overline{\mix_H 1_K} = \overline{1_K} = \frac{1}{[G:K]}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Constancy is a coset argument needing no character theory: for $a,b\in G$ write $ab^{-1} = h_0k$ with $h_0\in H$, $k\in K$; translating the summation index by $h_0$ matches the two sums term by term, since $1_K$ is invariant under right multiplication by $k$. A constant profile equals its own mean, which is $1/[G:K]$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:countblind}; Lemma~\ref{lem:const} finishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The multi-prime dial never dies]\label{thm:multiprime}
Let $[G:K]=2$. For every $k \ge 2$, $\Phi_k(1_K) = h(2^{-k}) - \tfrac12 h(2^{-(k-1)}) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
As in Theorem~\ref{thm:orderd}, with $x = 2^{-(k-1)} \le 1/2$: the upper bound on $h(x)$ and the lower bound on $h(x/2)$ leave the residual $2^{-k}\log 2 - O(4^{-k}) > 0$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $\Phi_k(1_K) = 0.21576,\,0.09560,\,0.04541,\,0.02216,\,0.01095$ for $k=2,\dots,6$: geometric decay, but never zero. Combined with $\Phi_k(\mix_G s) = 0$ and $\overline{\mix_G s} = \bar{s}$, at every number of prime factors the quadratic-character profile carries strictly positive information, its randomised version exactly none, and the count statistic cannot tell them apart.

\section{The budgeted multiplier adversary}

\begin{definition}[Two-part]
For a finite group $G$ let $\tau(G) = 2^{v_2(|G|)}$, the largest power of two dividing $|G|$---equivalently the order of a Sylow $2$-subgroup.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Odd index equals full $2$-part]\label{thm:twopart}
For a finite group $G$ and $H \le G$: $[G:H]$ is odd if and only if $\tau(G) \mid |H|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|H|\cdot[G:H] = |G|$, so $v_2(|G|) = v_2(|H|) + v_2([G:H])$; the index is odd iff $v_2([G:H])=0$ iff $v_2(|H|) = v_2(|G|)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Budget criterion]\label{thm:budgetcrit}
For a finite group $G$ and $B \in \mathbb{N}$: every $H \le G$ with $|H|\le B$ has even index if and only if $B < \tau(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) An odd-index subgroup would satisfy $|H| \ge \tau(G) > B$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:twopart}. ($\Rightarrow$) Sylow's theorem gives $H$ with $|H| = \tau(G)$, within budget when $B \ge \tau(G)$, and of odd index.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Budgeted dial dichotomy]\label{thm:budgeted}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian class group and $B\in\mathbb{N}$. Every multiplier group $H$ with $|H| \le B$ admits an admissible $H$-invariant profile attaining $C^{*}$ if and only if $B < \tau(G) = 2^{v_2(|G|)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:parity} with Theorem~\ref{thm:budgetcrit}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The attack at threshold]
If $B \ge \tau(G)$ there is a single multiplier group $H$ with $|H|\le B$---any Sylow $2$-subgroup---such that for every admissible profile $s$, $T(\mix_H s) < C^{*}$ while $\overline{\mix_H s} = \bar{s}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Size is no defence]
If $|G| = 2m$ with $m$ odd then $\tau(G)=2$, and a budget of $2$ already breaks the channel, however large $m$ is.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Unit groups]
On $(\mathbb{Z}/p)^{\times}$ the surviving-budget threshold is the $2$-part of $p-1$: threshold $16$ for $p=17$; $8$ for $p=41$; $4$ for $p=13,29,37$; and the minimum $2$ for $p = 11,19,23,31$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\subsection*{Exact evaluation of the dial}
Given $G = \mathbb{Z}/m_1 \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}/m_r$, a profile $s$ and $k \ge 2$: compute $\bar{s}$ in $O(|G|)$; compute $s^{\star k}$ by $k-1$ convolutions, at cost $O(|G|^{2})$ each directly or $O(|G|\log|G|)$ each via the fast Fourier transform on the character group, giving $O(k|G|\log|G|)$ overall; return $h(\bar{s}^{\,k}) - |G|^{-1}\sum_c h(s^{\star k}(c))$. Entropies must use $h(0)=h(1)=0$, and FFT output should be clipped to $[0,1]$ to absorb rounding.

\subsection*{Estimating the dial from a sample}
One observes pairs (class of composite, usable/not) and replaces $N_s(c)$ by the empirical pass rate within class $c$ and $\bar{s}^{\,2}$ by the overall empirical pass rate. Plug-in mutual information is biased upward by roughly $(|G|-1)/(2n)$ nats for $n$ samples; since the signal is bounded by $C^{*}\approx 0.216$ nats, the bin count must be small relative to $n$. Theorem~\ref{thm:inflation} is precisely the licence to quotient the class group down to a small character quotient---naturally the quotient by squares---without changing the estimand. The same invariance justifies pooling measurements taken at different bit-lengths and regimes, since they estimate one population quantity.

\subsection*{Evaluating the design threshold}
Given the class number $|G|$, the surviving-budget threshold of Theorem~\ref{thm:budgeted} is $\tau(G) = 2^{v_2(|G|)}$: strip factors of $2$ from $|G|$. This costs $O(\log|G|)$ and needs no knowledge of the group structure beyond its order.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the theory explains}
Each experimental observation has an exact counterpart. \emph{Stability across the grid:} by Theorems~\ref{thm:inflation} and \ref{thm:cell} the dial is a function of the character quotient, so enlarging the ambient class group and relabelling or translating it leave it fixed; the four measured correlations are four estimates of one invariant. \emph{Dial beats count:} Theorem~\ref{thm:separation} exhibits pairs with identical mean rate whose dials differ by the full $C^{*}$, so the empirical margin is a diluted version of a total separation. \emph{Multiplier randomisation destroys the channel:} by Theorems~\ref{thm:orth}, \ref{thm:flatten} and \ref{thm:parity}, averaging equidistributes the character and a single multiplier outside the kernel already sends the dial to exactly zero while the mean rate is preserved; the fixed-multiplier requirement is the case $H=\{1\}$ of the parity criterion.

\subsection{Limitations}
The model is idealised in three respects. First, the class of a candidate is modelled as uniform on $G$ and independent across factors; real samplers are mildly non-uniform and weakly dependent. Second, multiplier randomisation is modelled as averaging over a \emph{subgroup}; a practical multiplier set need not be one, and Theorem~\ref{thm:endpoints} shows that only subgroup averaging is confined to the endpoints. Third, all theorems concern the population quantity $T(s)$, whereas reported rank correlations are properties of an estimator on finite samples.

\subsection{Relation to classical facts}
Several ingredients are classical: character orthogonality over a subgroup, Sylow existence and Cauchy's theorem, multiplicativity of the $2$-adic valuation across the index formula, and strict concavity of the binary entropy. What appears to be new is the combination: an information-theoretic functional on profiles over a class group whose extremal set is exactly the quadratic characters, whose invariance group is exactly the inflations, and whose destruction under averaging is governed exactly by the $2$-part.

\subsection{Open problems}

\begin{problem}[Fourier bound for arbitrary profiles]
Theorem~\ref{thm:onedim} computes the dial exactly along the character family. For a general admissible profile, is there a bound $T(s) \le F\bigl(\max_{\psi\ne 1}|\hat{s}(\psi)|\bigr)$ with $F$ the natural analogue of $D$? Theorem~\ref{thm:continuum} shows such a bound would be tight along the contrast family.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Non-subgroup multiplier sets]
Characterise the achievable pairs (mean rate, dial) when the multiplier set is an arbitrary finite subset of $G$. The endpoints are settled; the interior is not.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Multi-prime degradation law]
Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:onedim} to $\Phi_k$: presumably the conditional profile of $s_t$ is $(1+t^{k}\chi(c))/2^{k}$, giving a $k$-indexed family of laws with contrast exponent $k$.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Adaptive adversaries]
Theorem~\ref{thm:budgeted} assumes a static multiplier group. If the adversary may adapt its multiplier distribution across rounds under a total budget, what is the optimal schedule, and does the $2$-part threshold survive?
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Estimator theory tuned to the invariance]
Design an estimator of $T$ that first estimates the character quotient and then estimates the dial on it, and quantify the variance reduction relative to the plug-in estimator on the full class group.
\end{problem}

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Result} & \textbf{Statement} \\
\midrule
Inflation invariance & $T(s\circ f) = T(s)$, $\Phi_k(s\circ f)=\Phi_k(s)$ for surjective $f$ \\
Intersection cell & Every inflated index-two coset indicator has dial exactly $C^{*}$ \\
Count blindness & $\overline{\mix_H s} = \bar{s}$ for all $H$, $s$ \\
Washout dichotomy & Cap attainable under $H$ iff $H$ lies in an index-two subgroup \\
Parity criterion & Equivalently, iff $[G:H]$ is even \\
Character collapse & $H \not\le K$, $[G:K]=2$ $\Rightarrow$ $\mix_H 1_K \equiv \tfrac12$, $T=0$ \\
Count-blind separation & Same mean $\tfrac12$, dials differing by $C^{*}$ \\
Degradation law & $T((1+t\chi)/2) = D(t^{2})$, strictly increasing, $D(0)=0$, $D(1)=C^{*}$ \\
Order-$d$ channels & $T(1_K) = h(1/d^{2}) - \tfrac1d h(1/d) > 0$; collapse to $0$ if $H\vee K = G$ \\
Multi-prime & $\Phi_k(1_K) = h(2^{-k}) - \tfrac12 h(2^{-(k-1)}) > 0$; $\Phi_k(\mix_G s) = 0$ \\
Budgeted adversary & Survival for all $|H|\le B$ iff $B < 2^{v_2(|G|)}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\medskip

\noindent Numerical values: $C^{*} = 0.2157615543$ nats $=0.3112781245$ bits; $T(1_K)$ for $d=2,\dots,6$ equals $0.21576$, $0.13666$, $0.09321$, $0.06786$, $0.05184$; $\Phi_k(1_K)$ for $k=2,\dots,6$ equals $0.21576$, $0.09560$, $0.04541$, $0.02216$, $0.01095$; $D(t^{2})$ at $t=0.5,0.8,0.9$ equals $0.01050$, $0.07264$, $0.12248$.

